HIT Changes in minimum fibrous cap thickness (FCT) (+51.6 ± 40.9 vs. +12.4 ± 23.9 μm; P < 0.001) and lipid arc (-60.9 ± 56.5° vs. -9.1 ± 70.8°; P = 0.008) were greater in the high Lp(a) group with evolocumab compared with placebo.
HIT Among patients with low Lp(a) levels, evolocumab produced lower levels of LDL-C (23.3 ± 34.9 vs. 82.9 ± 46.5 mg/dL; P < 0.001) and Lp(a) [11.5 (5.8, 23.8) vs. 25.0 (13.5, 41.0) nmol/L; P = 0.01] compared with placebo, but no differences were observed between groups in changes in minimum FCT (+45.9 ± 37.8 vs. +34.7 ± 36.0 μm; P = 0.21) and lipid arc (-59.9 ± 50.1° vs. -44.5 ± 46.1°; P = 0.18).

[02] 41369622 year=2026 nct= title=Obesity-Associated Cardiovascular Risk and Benefit From PCSK9 Inhibition: A Prespecified Analysis From FOURIER.
HIT BACKGROUND: The PCSK9 inhibitor evolocumab decreases the risk of major adverse cardiovascular events (MACE).
HIT OBJECTIVES: This study sought to investigate the association between BMI, risk of MACE, and the clinical benefit of evolocumab.
HIT METHODS: The FOURIER (Further Cardiovascular Outcomes Research with PCSK9 Inhibition in Subjects with Elevated Risk) trial randomized 27,564 stable atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease patients to evolocumab or placebo (median follow-up 2.2 years).
HIT The primary endpoint was cardiovascular death, myocardial infarction, stroke, hospitalization for unstable angina, or coronary revascularization.
HIT The association between BMI and cardiovascular risk was examined in the placebo arm adjusting for clinical predictors.
HIT In the placebo arm, for every 5-unit-higher BMI above 30 kg/m2, there was an 11% higher risk of the primary endpoint (adjusted HR: 1.11; 95% CI: 1.02-1.21).
HIT The relative risk reduction for the primary endpoint with evolocumab was progressively greater in those with BMI ≥30 kg/m2 modeled on a continuous basis (P for interaction = 0.025).
HIT Evolocumab reduced the risk of the primary endpoint by 11% in those with a BMI <30 kg/m2 (HR: 0.89; 95% CI: 0.81-0.98), by 14% in those with BMI of 30 to <35 kg/m2 (HR: 0.86; 95% CI: 0.75-0.98), and by 29% in those with BMI ≥35 kg/m2 (HR: 0.71; 95% CI: 0.59-0.86).

